Linear Regression – Instagram Usage vs Phone Battery  

Task 1: Scatter Plot of Data

Code:
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
instagram_hours = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7])
battery_percentage = np.array([90, 82, 75, 65, 58, 48, 40])
plt.scatter(instagram_hours, battery_percentage)
plt.xlabel("Instagram Usage (Hours)")
plt.ylabel("Battery Percentage (%)")
plt.title("Instagram Usage vs Phone Battery")
plt.show()

Answer:
Scatter plot shows the relationship between Instagram usage (hours) and phone battery percentage at day’s end.

Task 2: Fit Linear Regression Model

Code:
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
X = instagram_hours.reshape(-1, 1)
y = battery_percentage
model = LinearRegression()
model.fit(X, y)
print("Slope (Coefficient):", model.coef_[0])
print("Intercept:", model.intercept_)

Answer:
- Slope (Coefficient): Change in battery % for each extra hour on Instagram.  
- Intercept: Predicted battery % when Instagram usage = 0 hours.  

Task 3: Plot Regression Line

Code:
y_pred = model.predict(X)
plt.scatter(instagram_hours, battery_percentage, label="Actual Data")
plt.plot(instagram_hours, y_pred, label="Regression Line")
plt.xlabel("Instagram Usage (Hours)")
plt.ylabel("Battery Percentage (%)")
plt.title("Instagram Usage vs Phone Battery")
plt.legend()
plt.show()

Explain:
Scatter points = actual data.  
Regression line = predicted battery % from the model.  

Task 4: Calculate Mean Squared Error (MSE)

Code:
from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error
mse = mean_squared_error(y, y_pred)
print("Mean Squared Error (MSE):", mse)

Explain:  
- MSE -- measures prediction error.  
- Lower MSE → predictions are closer to actual values.  

Complete Code (All Tasks Together)

import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error
instagram_hours = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7])
battery_percentage = np.array([90, 82, 75, 65, 58, 48, 40])
plt.scatter(instagram_hours, battery_percentage)
plt.xlabel("Instagram Usage (Hours)")
plt.ylabel("Battery Percentage (%)")
plt.title("Instagram Usage vs Phone Battery")
plt.show()
X = instagram_hours.reshape(-1, 1)
y = battery_percentage
model = LinearRegression()
model.fit(X, y)
print("Slope (Coefficient):", model.coef_[0])
print("Intercept:", model.intercept_)
y_pred = model.predict(X)
plt.scatter(instagram_hours, battery_percentage, label="Actual Data")
plt.plot(instagram_hours, y_pred, label="Regression Line")
plt.xlabel("Instagram Usage (Hours)")
plt.ylabel("Battery Percentage (%)")
plt.title("Instagram Usage vs Phone Battery")
plt.legend()
plt.show()
mse = mean_squared_error(y, y_pred)
print("Mean Squared Error (MSE):", mse)
print("Lower MSE indicates predictions are close to actual values.")
